**`src/lib/capabilities.functions.ts:139-148` + `153-211`** — `buildCapabilityForAgent` is called in a serial loop over `activeCast`, and inside it, three queries (`loadVoiceAnchorBlock(userId)`, `workspace_briefs`, `brief_items`) are scoped only to `userId`/`workspaceId` — **identical on every iteration** — yet re-run per agent. For a 13-agent cast that's 39 round trips for 3 rows. Same shape as the workspace-context cache you just added to `loop.server.ts` — hoist these above the loop, same pattern as the already-hoisted `trustByAgentId`:

```ts
// in buildCapabilities, before the for-loop:
const voiceBlock = await loadVoiceAnchorBlock(supabase, userId).catch(() => "");
let briefBlock = "", briefItemsBlock = "";
if (workspaceId) {
  const [{ data: brief }, { data: briefItems }] = await Promise.all([
    supabase.from("workspace_briefs").select("...").eq("workspace_id", workspaceId).maybeSingle(),
    supabase.from("brief_items").select("...").eq("workspace_id", workspaceId).eq("status", "standing"),
  ]);
  briefBlock = renderBriefBlock(brief as any);
  briefItemsBlock = renderBriefItemsBlock(briefItems as BriefItem[] | null);
}
// pass voiceBlock/briefBlock/briefItemsBlock into buildCapabilityForAgent instead of loading them inside it
```

**`src/lib/ai/eval-runner.server.ts:144-227`** — cases are independent but run in a `for` loop with a per-case `eval_case_results` insert. Parallelize with the repo's established `pLimit` pattern and batch the insert:

```ts
import pLimit from "p-limit";
const limit = pLimit(5);
const rowsToInsert: Array<Record<string, unknown>> = [];
await Promise.all(cases.map((c) => limit(async () => {
  // ...existing subject/judge logic...
  rowsToInsert.push({ run_id: runId, case_id: c.id, /* ... */ });
})));
await supabase.from("eval_case_results").insert(rowsToInsert);
```

**`src/lib/lineage.functions.ts:353-362`** — `promotePrdToTasks` calls `recordLineage` (a single-row `upsert`) once per task in a loop. `recordLineage`'s `upsert` accepts an array with the same `onConflict`, so batch it directly instead of importing the per-row helper:

```ts
if (tasks?.length) {
  await supabase.from("artifact_lineage").upsert(
    tasks.map((t) => ({
      user_id: userId,
      parent_kind: "prd", parent_id: prd.id,
      child_kind: "task", child_id: t.id,
      relation: "promoted",
      rationale: "Generated from PRD by promotePrdToTasks",
      created_by_agent: "prd-writer",
    })),
    { onConflict: "user_id,parent_kind,parent_id,child_kind,child_id,relation" },
  );
}
```

**`src/components/discover/OpportunityQueue.tsx:239-260` vs `363-385`** — the comment at line 239 claims "stable callback handlers to prevent re-renders," and the handlers genuinely are `useCallback`'d and take `id` as a parameter. But the call site never uses that — it still wraps every one in a fresh arrow per row: `onOpen={() => handleOpen(o.id)}`, `onChallenge={() => handleChallenge(o.id)}`, etc. (lines 371-384). That new closure defeats `OpportunityRow`'s `memo()` exactly as if the `useCallback` layer didn't exist. This is the sibling files' actual fix pattern (id-based handlers passed straight through, id read inside the child) — apply it here too:

```tsx
// OpportunityRow needs an `id` prop and calls onOpen(id) internally instead of
// the parent pre-binding it. Call site becomes:
<OpportunityRow
  id={o.id}
  onOpen={handleOpen}          // was onOpen={() => handleOpen(o.id)}
  onChallenge={handleChallenge}
  onDraftSpec={handleDraftSpec}
  onLineage={handleLineage}
  onSetStatus={handleSetStatus}   // signature already takes (id, status)
  onDelete={handleDelete}         // signature already takes (id, title) — pass title too or read via id
  ...
/>
```

**`src/components/obsidian/AskPanel.tsx:1044-1053`** — the stream-abort effect only runs its cleanup logic when `isOpen` flips to `false`:
```ts
React.useEffect(() => {
  if (isOpen) return;
  if (abortControllerRef.current) { abortControllerRef.current.abort(); ... }
  readAloud.stop();
}, [isOpen, readAloud]);
```
If the component unmounts by some *other* path while `isOpen` is still `true` (parent stops rendering `AskPanel`, an onboarding flip, a route change), this effect's own unmount doesn't fire that body — it only runs on the next render where `isOpen` has changed. Add a true unmount-only cleanup:
```ts
React.useEffect(() => {
  return () => {
    abortControllerRef.current?.abort();
    abortControllerRef.current = null;
  };
}, []); // empty deps: fires only on unmount, independent of the isOpen-driven effect above
```

**`src/components/knowledge/CalendarPanel.tsx:316-360`** — `meetingItems`, `eventItems`, `allItems`, `feed` (filter+sort), `pastCount` (filter), and `buckets` (groupBy) are all recomputed inline on every render, unmemoized, off react-query data that only actually changes on refetch:
```ts
const allItems = useMemo(
  () => [...meetingItems(meetings.data), ...eventItems(events.data)],
  [meetings.data, events.data],
);
const feed = useMemo(() => allItems.filter(...).sort(...), [allItems]);
const pastCount = useMemo(() => allItems.filter(...).length, [allItems]);
const buckets = useMemo(() => { /* groupBy */ }, [allItems]);
```

---

Want me to implement these six? They follow the exact patterns already committed today (`pLimit`, batch upsert, hoisted queries, id-based handlers, unmount-only effect, `useMemo`), so it'd be quick — happy to knock them out and commit under BUILD-ONLY mode like the rest of today's fixes.

